ef 1053 #uuid "91d90bfa-8f19-5546-aba3-f391b0f30b02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aff78f44-088e-5944-87f1-2279a4c5e2ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125132 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5471fadb-1b86-5cb7-bba6-ebb075794d52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125133 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40210" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ y-x+6z \\\\ 8z-x+3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f485de0-d115-59d2-87d8-fc0e53ab516c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125150 #edb/ref 1074 "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125401 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125401 #edb/ref 1061 20015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125401 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42af34b-8f66-5aee-9bd5-7a3de03f5cdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125401 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125402 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125402 #edb/ref 1061 20016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125402 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ddfd46cc-2853-59ef-ace0-92fabfa7af70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125402 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124519 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125403 #edb/ref 1061 20017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125403 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb409d13-a534-5413-97fd-7e71ad7fbfad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125403 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125448 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125448 #edb/ref 1061 20424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125448 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "905da979-387e-5ded-9c78-3ede6dac59ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125448 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1061 25971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb766bed-08ee-564c-a45c-c20f57e59be3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125490 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125497 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125497 #edb/ref 1061 26848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125497 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f45ab6b-4ad9-5e42-8a3f-69ef72e1a03c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125497 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125783 #edb/ref 1064 "The **image** (or **range**) of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is the set of all vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ that the transformation maps to. That is to say\n![](images/4c/4c89132f0fd9713f0e688f7cf00189bf4d3f27baddad032c74edf7b0a8c0d8ee.svg)\n\nThe diagram below gives a schematic representation of the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: V \\rightarrow V$ for a vector space $V.$\n\n![](images/b8/b859250756eb3870f9c1d4d7e6e78f044adf2b0c4cd94a87d01011879d668afc.png)\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{0}$ is always in the image of a linear transformation since $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{0})=\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the linear transformation\n![](images/61/618376922e3c837dba3f742fd77340c2a3deee6cee099843232f85bcec5faf93.svg)\n\nNotice that, for example, the vector $\\mathbf b = [6,9]^T$ is in the image of the transformation $\\mathbf{S}$ since there is another vector $\\mathbf{u} = [1,1]^T$ such that $\\mathbf{S}(\\mathbf{u})=\\mathbf{b}{:}$\n\n![](images/51/51004801582ad1a6cddc389e07a4ac8d486c410ebacf264de14ac1a798a10c30.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125783 #edb/ref 1065 3530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125783 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "70bccafd-705b-5026-bdf8-ef7731dfcc77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125783 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125819 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the image of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equivalent to the column space of its standard matrix $T.$ That is,\n![](images/6e/6e8b0992ade86b8e8a0c7d2227ff5473f5cae843f8e763e556c17654e7f1f143.svg)\n\nSo to find out if a vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is in the image of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we just need to check if the matrix equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125819 #edb/ref 1065 4276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125819 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f52415b1-727f-53a7-8502-857fdcb3b835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125819 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining If a Vector Lies in the Image" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, the rank of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is equal to the dimension of its image:\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T}))\n$$\n\nIf the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ has the standard matrix $T,$ then the dimension of the image is equal to the rank of $T{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T)\n$$\n\nSo, in general, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{rank}(T).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1065 4382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "89b10851-3eed-5f36-a0ab-c9d0610e9b75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125821 #edb/ref 1067 "The Rank of a Linear Transformation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269320 #edb/ref 1080 "-24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269320 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269320 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05afaaf0-89b1-5ce4-a384-1ae117b7f130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218