mine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$  belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "701a2c58-508c-5dda-9dee-75bbafc3bc3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272018 #edb/ref 1074 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2x-4y \\\\ x-3y \\\\ -x+4y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=2 \\\\ x-3y=7 \\\\ -x+4y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=7+3y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =2 \\\\ 2(7+3y)-4y & =2 \\\\ 2y & =-12 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7+3y \\\\  & =7+3(-6) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =k \\\\ -(-11)+4(-6) & =k \\\\ k & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-13.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186271678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1071 "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ -11{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7679a9d1-7c16-5065-b275-88941dd34726" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272019 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321151 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186321151 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #